  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (Through Sunday)...\n\nVigorous upper trough will be tracking into and across our region\novernight and through today. Currently, upper low is centered \nover NW Montana with 100kt 300 mb jet moving up into southwestern\nMontana. Scattered showers and thunderstorms had developed over\nportions of southeastern Montana in the warm sector ahead of this\nsystem and will affect the far east overnight. In the meantime, \nother widespread showers, and possibly embedded thunder, were\ndeveloping over our western zones and these will slide eastward \nthrough the rest of the night. This area will be powered by the \ncold front and strong upper dynamics associated with this system. \nWe expect to see a few elongated bands of moderate precipitation \nwithin broader areas of light showers. This activity may also \ngenerate a period of locally strong NW winds (30-45 mph) as it \nmoves east/northeast overnight.\n\nAs the upper low tracks across the MT/Canadian border today, the\nbetter forcing will lift east of our CWA by mid to late morning. \nAt that point the more significant impact with this system becomes\nthe strong westerly winds across our region. Widespread westerly\nwind gusts of 35 to 55 mph can be expected, with the greatest \nwind gusts along the western foothills and out east near the \nDakotas border (Baker area). Locations from Big Timber north to \nJudith Gap have roughly a 40-70% chance at seeing wind gusts of 50\nmph or greater (morning), with similar probabilities near the \nDakotas border (afternoon) thanks to 700 mb wind max moving over \nthe region. Cannot completely rule out a local gust or two near \n60 mph, but majority of peak gusts should be sub-warning criteria.\nThus, no highlights at this time. As winds drop and skies clear \nout Monday night, slight chance of frost is possible for some of \nthe colder valley locations with temperatures dropping into the \n30s and lower 40s.\n\nEnsembles suggest another upper low developing over the Pacific \nNW by midweek while zonal flow, or at best low amplitude ridging,\ndevelops over our region. By late week, energy in that west coast\ntrough ejects into the Rockies and it gradually begins to move\neast, pushing some moisture into our region from the south. So \nlook for generally seasonable temperatures through the week with \na chance of precipitation starting Thursday and increasing into \nthe weekend. BT",
